<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify familiar vocabulary from daily life used in context. Interactive: Engage in short exchanges using phrases learned from classroom discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify and respond accurately to familiar vocabulary from daily life when used in discussions (e.g., "What animal is slow?" "The sloth."). Can engage with familiar phrases about animals and nature (e.g., "Where does the sloth live?" "It lives in the rainforest."). Can demonstrate comprehension of numbers or quantities associated with familiar topics when spoken slowly and clearly (e.g., "three fish" or "five trees").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts with an animal sound game. The teacher plays recordings of rainforest animal sounds (e.g., monkey, parrot, frog) and asks students to guess the animal by pointing to corresponding flashcards or acting out. "What animal is this? Listen!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher then shows a large, colorful picture of a sloth hanging from a branch in the rainforest. "Look! What is this? It's an animal! It's a rainforest animal!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a sloth and says slowly and clearly: "Sloth. It's a sloth. A slow sloth." The teacher models the /s/ sound in "sloth." Then, the teacher shows a picture of a rainforest and says: "Rainforest. It's the rainforest." The teacher models the /r/ sound in "rainforest." Students repeat chorally and individually. The teacher uses TPR by moving very slowly to demonstrate "slow." The teacher introduces "furry" by pointing to the sloth's fur. "It's furry."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks simple yes/no and WH-questions to check understanding, encouraging students to point or give one-word answers.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the sloth fast or slow?" (Expected answer: "Slow.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the sloth live in the ocean? Or the rainforest?" (Expected answer: "Rainforest.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a cat or a sloth?" (Pointing to sloth flashcard. Expected answer: "Sloth.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio story (or teacher-read narration) accompanied by visual flashcards depicting the key animals and habitat. The story will introduce "sloth," "rainforest," "jungle," "monkey," "toucan," "frog," and adjectives "slow," "furry," "colorful."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can identify familiar vocabulary from daily life used in context and engage in short exchanges using phrases learned from classroom discussions. (Receptive and Interactive Listening)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher plays the audio story (or reads it slowly and clearly) while displaying flashcards for "rainforest," "jungle," "sloth," "monkey," "toucan," "frog." The teacher pauses after each sentence to point to the corresponding visual and repeat key words. "This is the rainforest. Look! It's a slow, furry sloth. The sloth lives in the jungle. A colorful toucan flies. A jumpy monkey swings. A green frog jumps." The teacher emphasizes /dʒ/ in "jungle."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist Questions):</b> Students listen and respond by pointing to flashcards or giving short answers.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal is the story mostly about?" (Teacher repeats: "Sloth?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where does the sloth live? In the rainforest or the city?" (Teacher repeats: "Rainforest?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the toucan colorful?" (Teacher points to toucan. Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying animals and their characteristics through listening. Focusing on: Adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, furry monkey, colorful toucan, green frog). WH-questions ("What animal is it?", "Where does it live?"). Present simple ("It lives in the rainforest."). Vocabulary: sloth, rainforest, jungle, monkey, toucan, frog, slow, furry, colorful.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher lays out flashcards of the target animals (sloth, monkey, toucan, frog) and a rainforest scene on the table. The teacher says a word or a short phrase, and students listen and point to the correct flashcard. "Point to the sloth." "Point to the rainforest." "Point to the colorful toucan." "Point to the furry monkey." The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of /s/, /r/, /dʒ/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher describes an animal using 1-2 adjectives and its habitat. Students listen and say the animal's name or point to it. "Listen! It's slow and furry. What animal is it?" (Expected: "Sloth!") "It's colorful and flies. What animal is it?" (Expected: "Toucan!") "It lives in the jungle. It is jumpy." (Expected: "Monkey!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Respond (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of flashcards. Student A (teacher role) says a simple sentence: "It's a slow sloth." or "This is the rainforest." Student B (listener role) points to the correct flashcard. Then they swap roles. The teacher monitors closely, correcting pronunciation and modeling again if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Rainforest Explorer Game" – a listening comprehension game where students respond non-verbally or with single words to descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher acts as the "Rainforest Tour Guide." Students sit in a circle or at their desks with flashcards spread out in front of them (sloth, monkey, toucan, frog, rainforest, jungle). The teacher says: "Welcome, rainforest explorers! Let's find some animals! Listen carefully."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I see a very slow animal. It is furry. What is it?" (Students listen and point to the sloth flashcard or say "Sloth!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! It has colorful feathers and it flies! What animal is it?" (Students listen and point to the toucan flashcard or say "Toucan!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where do these animals live? In the rainforest or the desert?" (Students listen and point to the rainforest flashcard or say "Rainforest!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This animal is jumpy and likes to swing on vines. What is it?" (Students listen and point to the monkey flashcard or say "Monkey!")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reinforces respectful language for nature: "The sloth is quiet. It lives in the jungle." "The rainforest is beautiful."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Rainforest Explorer Game" and a final individual listening identification task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will complete a short "Listen and Circle" worksheet. The teacher will say a word or a short description (e.g., "The slow sloth," "The colorful toucan," "This is the rainforest"), and students listen and circle the corresponding picture from a choice of three (e.g., sloth, monkey, toucan, or rainforest, city, ocean). Includes one question about quantity: "Circle three frogs."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies target vocabulary (sloth, rainforest, monkey, toucan, frog) when heard clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds accurately to simple descriptions (e.g., "slow and furry" for sloth).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of numbers or quantities (e.g., "three frogs").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Before leaving, students are asked to show their understanding:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the animal that is slow." (Students point to a sloth flashcard or picture.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound does a monkey make?" (Students make a monkey sound or point to monkey picture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new animal word did you hear today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you hear the /s/ sound in 'sloth'?" (Students give a thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in introducing the new vocabulary and listening tasks? Students responded well to TPR and the animal sounds. The constant repetition and visual cues were effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still confused "jungle" and "rainforest" despite the /dʒ/ sound practice. More specific context or distinguishing features for these two might be needed. I should also prepare more activities to encourage even the quieter students to point or give one-word answers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core rainforest animals and their characteristics through listening. The next lesson will use these same vocabulary words in simple written contexts to develop reading comprehension, building on the visual and auditory recognition established today. Students will read short sentences about the animals they heard about.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>